INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','easy','mcq','A 68-year-old woman presents with knee pain worse with activity and relieved by rest. X-ray shows joint space narrowing and bony outgrowths at the joint margins. Which pathological process best describes the bony outgrowths seen in osteoarthritis?','["A. Osteophyte formation","B. Pannus formation","C. Syndesmophyte formation","D. Tophus deposition"]','A','Osteophytes are bony outgrowths that form at the joint margins in osteoarthritis due to attempted repair of damaged cartilage. They differ from pannus (granulation tissue in RA), syndesmophytes (vertical ossifications in AS), and tophi (urate deposits in gout).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','medium','mcq','A pathologist examining a resected femoral head from a patient with severe osteoarthritis notes areas of dense, polished bone with an ivory-like appearance at the articular surface. What is this finding called?','["A. Subchondral sclerosis","B. Eburnation","C. Fibrillation","D. Chondrocalcinosis"]','B','Eburnation refers to the ivory-like polished appearance of exposed subchondral bone in advanced osteoarthritis, occurring when overlying articular cartilage is completely eroded. Fibrillation is early cartilage surface fraying. Subchondral sclerosis is reactive bone thickening. Chondrocalcinosis is calcium crystal deposition.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','medium','mcq','Which cytokines are primarily responsible for driving matrix metalloproteinase production and cartilage degradation in osteoarthritis?','["A. IL-4 and IL-10","B. IL-17 and IL-23","C. IL-1 and TNF-alpha","D. IL-6 and IL-12"]','C','IL-1 and TNF-alpha are the principal pro-inflammatory cytokines in osteoarthritis that stimulate chondrocytes to produce matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs), leading to degradation of type II collagen and aggrecan in articular cartilage. IL-4/IL-10 are anti-inflammatory; IL-17/IL-23 are more prominent in psoriatic arthritis; IL-6/IL-12 have lesser roles.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','easy','mcq','Hard nodules at the distal interphalangeal joints of a 72-year-old woman with osteoarthritis are noted on examination. What are these nodules called?','["A. Bouchard''s nodes","B. Rheumatoid nodules","C. Heberden''s nodes","D. Tophi"]','D','Heberden''s nodes are bony enlargements (osteophytes) at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints in osteoarthritis. Bouchard''s nodes occur at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints. Rheumatoid nodules are subcutaneous granulomas in RA. Tophi are urate crystal deposits in gout.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','easy','mcq','Which joints are most commonly affected in primary osteoarthritis due to mechanical loading?','["A. Shoulder and elbow joints","B. Hip, knee, and lumbar spine","C. Metacarpophalangeal joints","D. Sacroiliac joints"]','A','The hip, knee, and lumbar spine are the weight-bearing joints most commonly affected in primary osteoarthritis due to repetitive mechanical stress. The shoulder, elbow, and MCP joints are less commonly affected unless there is secondary OA from injury or metabolic disease. Sacroiliac joints are characteristic of ankylosing spondylitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','medium','mcq','On histological examination of articular cartilage from a patient with early osteoarthritis, the surface shows vertical clefts and fraying. What is this process called?','["A. Eburnation","B. Fibrillation","C. Chondroid metaplasia","D. Pannus formation"]','B','Fibrillation is the earliest histologic change in osteoarthritic cartilage, characterized by superficial vertical clefts and fraying of the articular surface due to disruption of the collagen fibril network. It precedes eburnation (which occurs after cartilage is completely lost). Pannus formation is characteristic of RA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','hard','mcq','A 70-year-old man with chronic knee osteoarthritis undergoes MRI which reveals fluid-filled cavities in the subchondral bone. What is the pathogenesis of these subchondral cysts?','["A. Synovial fluid is forced into microfractures under pressure creating cystic spaces","B. Avascular necrosis of subchondral trabeculae creates necrotic foci","C. Calcium pyrophosphate crystals dissolve bone creating cavities","D. Inflammatory pannus erodes into subchondral bone"]','C','Subchondral cysts in osteoarthritis form when synovial fluid is forced through microfractures in the damaged articular cartilage and subchondral bone under loading pressure, creating fluid-filled cavities. This is not avascular necrosis, crystal dissolution, or pannus erosion (the latter being characteristic of RA).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','medium','mcq','Which of the following best distinguishes primary osteoarthritis from secondary osteoarthritis?','["A. Primary OA affects weight-bearing joints; secondary OA affects non-weight-bearing joints","B. Primary OA has no identifiable predisposing condition; secondary OA results from a known underlying cause","C. Primary OA occurs in young patients; secondary OA occurs in elderly patients","D. Primary OA shows significant synovial inflammation; secondary OA does not"]','D','Primary (idiopathic) OA has no identifiable predisposing condition and occurs in otherwise normal joints, typically in older patients. Secondary OA results from a known underlying condition such as prior joint injury, metabolic disease (hemochromatosis, Wilson''s disease), congenital deformity, or previous inflammatory arthritis. Both can affect any joint.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','hard','mcq','A rheumatologist notes that a patient with osteoarthritis has minimal synovial fluid WBC count (<2000 cells/mm3) compared to a patient with rheumatoid arthritis (>50,000 cells/mm3). Which statement about synovial changes in OA is most accurate per Robbins pathology?','["A. OA shows exuberant synovial hyperplasia with pannus formation identical to RA","B. OA synovium shows mild synovial hyperplasia and mild chronic inflammation without pannus","C. OA is characterized by complete absence of any synovial changes","D. OA synovium shows fibrinoid necrosis and neutrophilic infiltrate"]','A','In osteoarthritis, the synovium shows mild hyperplasia of synoviocytes and mild chronic inflammatory infiltrate, but this is far less pronounced than in RA and pannus does not form. The inflammatory changes in OA are secondary to cartilage debris shedding into the joint. OA is primarily a degenerative rather than inflammatory disease, though low-grade inflammation does occur.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoarthritis','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old obese woman is diagnosed with bilateral knee osteoarthritis. Her physician recommends weight loss. Which mechanism best explains why obesity accelerates osteoarthritis progression?','["A. Obesity causes systemic hyperuricemia that deposits crystals in joints","B. Excess body weight increases mechanical loading on articular cartilage, promoting degradation","C. Adipose tissue releases anti-inflammatory adipokines that protect cartilage","D. Obesity promotes autoantibody production targeting type II collagen"]','B','Obesity accelerates osteoarthritis primarily through increased mechanical loading on weight-bearing joints, which promotes cartilage degradation. Additionally, adipokines (leptin, adiponectin) released from adipose tissue have pro-inflammatory effects. Hyperuricemia can cause gout but is not the mechanism in OA. OA is not an autoimmune disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with symmetric swelling of the proximal interphalangeal and metacarpophalangeal joints for 6 months, morning stiffness lasting 2 hours, and fatigue. Which antibody is most specific for rheumatoid arthritis?','["A. Rheumatoid factor (IgM)","B. Anti-nuclear antibody (ANA)","C. Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP)","D. Anti-double stranded DNA"]','C','Anti-CCP antibodies are the most specific serologic marker for rheumatoid arthritis (~95% specificity), and they can appear years before clinical disease onset. Rheumatoid factor (IgM anti-IgG) is sensitive but not specific (positive in other conditions). ANA is positive in many autoimmune diseases. Anti-dsDNA is specific for SLE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','hard','mcq','A biopsy of inflamed synovium from a patient with rheumatoid arthritis shows a thick layer of granulation tissue growing over and replacing the articular cartilage. What is this tissue called and what cells primarily drive its formation?','["A. Tophi; neutrophils and mast cells","B. Pannus; CD4+ T-helper cells, macrophages, and synoviocytes","C. Osteophyte; osteoblasts driven by BMP signaling","D. Sequestrum; neutrophils and osteoclasts"]','D','Pannus is the hallmark lesion of rheumatoid arthritis — a destructive layer of granulation tissue (synoviocytes, macrophages, fibroblasts) that invades and erodes articular cartilage and subchondral bone. CD4+ T-helper cells drive the process by releasing IL-1, TNF-alpha, and IL-17, activating macrophages and synoviocytes. Tophi occur in gout; osteophytes in OA; sequestrum in osteomyelitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','easy','mcq','A patient with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis is found to have splenomegaly and neutropenia in addition to joint disease. What is this triad called?','["A. Reiter syndrome","B. Sjogren syndrome","C. Felty syndrome","D. Still''s disease"]','A','Felty syndrome is the triad of rheumatoid arthritis + splenomegaly + neutropenia. It occurs in patients with severe, long-standing seropositive RA and can lead to recurrent infections. Reiter syndrome (reactive arthritis) involves arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis. Sjogren involves sicca symptoms. Still''s disease is systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','medium','mcq','X-ray of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis shows loss of joint space, periarticular osteopenia, and marginal erosions at the PIP and MCP joints. What is the radiographic hallmark that distinguishes RA from osteoarthritis?','["A. Osteophyte formation at joint margins","B. Marginal erosions with periarticular osteopenia","C. Subchondral sclerosis and cyst formation","D. Calcification of articular cartilage"]','B','Marginal erosions (bare area erosions) with periarticular osteopenia are the radiographic hallmarks of RA, reflecting pannus destruction at the edges of articular cartilage. Osteoarthritis shows osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and cysts without significant periarticular osteopenia. Chondrocalcinosis suggests CPPD/pseudogout.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','medium','mcq','Which HLA type is most strongly associated with rheumatoid arthritis and confers susceptibility through the shared epitope hypothesis?','["A. HLA-B27","B. HLA-Cw6","C. HLA-DR4","D. HLA-DR2"]','C','HLA-DR4 (and HLA-DR1) are associated with rheumatoid arthritis through the shared epitope — a conserved amino acid sequence in the peptide-binding groove that predisposes to presentation of citrullinated peptides to CD4+ T cells. HLA-B27 is associated with seronegative spondyloarthropathies. HLA-Cw6 with psoriasis. HLA-DR2/DR3 with SLE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','hard','mcq','A patient with rheumatoid arthritis develops progressive finger deformity in which the PIP joint is flexed and the DIP joint is hyperextended. What is this deformity called?','["A. Swan-neck deformity","B. Boutonniere deformity","C. Mallet finger","D. Ulnar drift"]','D','Boutonniere deformity consists of PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension, caused by rupture of the central slip of the extensor tendon at the PIP joint, allowing the lateral bands to slip volarly and act as flexors. Swan-neck deformity is the opposite (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion). Mallet finger involves DIP flexion from extensor tendon avulsion. Ulnar drift is MCP deviation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','medium','mcq','Histological examination of a subcutaneous nodule from a patient with seropositive RA shows a central zone of fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by a palisade of macrophages and fibroblasts with an outer layer of chronic inflammatory cells. What is this lesion?','["A. Tophus","B. Rheumatoid nodule","C. Granuloma annulare","D. Xanthoma"]','A','A rheumatoid nodule classically shows central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by a palisade of macrophages/histiocytes, with peripheral chronic inflammation. They occur in ~25% of RA patients, typically at pressure points (olecranon). A tophus shows amorphous urate crystals surrounded by a foreign-body giant cell reaction. Granuloma annulare lacks fibrinoid necrosis. Xanthomas contain lipid-laden macrophages.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','easy','mcq','Which extra-articular manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis most commonly affects the cardiovascular system?','["A. Coronary artery aneurysms","B. Libman-Sacks endocarditis","C. Pericarditis and pericardial effusion","D. Aortic stenosis from valve calcification"]','B','Pericarditis and pericardial effusion are the most common cardiovascular manifestations of rheumatoid arthritis, occurring in up to 50% of patients at autopsy though often subclinical. Libman-Sacks endocarditis is associated with SLE. Coronary artery aneurysms occur in Kawasaki disease. RA can cause valvular nodules but not aortic stenosis typically.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','hard','mcq','A researcher studying rheumatoid arthritis finds that citrullination of self-proteins is central to pathogenesis. Which enzyme converts arginine residues to citrulline in joint tissues?','["A. Cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2)","B. Peptidylarginine deiminase (PAD)","C. Matrix metalloproteinase-3 (MMP-3)","D. Cathepsin K"]','C','Peptidylarginine deiminase (PAD) enzymes, particularly PAD4, convert arginine to citrulline in proteins such as fibrinogen, vimentin, and collagen. These citrullinated proteins are recognized as foreign by the immune system in genetically susceptible individuals (HLA-DR4 shared epitope), triggering anti-CCP antibody production and T-cell responses. COX-2 mediates prostaglandin synthesis. MMP-3 degrades matrix. Cathepsin K is an osteoclast protease.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Rheumatoid Arthritis','medium','mcq','A patient with severe rheumatoid arthritis is started on a biologic agent that blocks TNF-alpha. Before starting this therapy, which infection must be screened for because TNF blockade can reactivate it?','["A. Hepatitis C virus","B. Cytomegalovirus","C. Herpes simplex virus","D. Latent Mycobacterium tuberculosis"]','D','TNF-alpha is critical for maintaining granuloma integrity in latent TB infection. Anti-TNF therapy (etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab) can reactivate latent TB by disrupting the granuloma, leading to potentially fatal disseminated TB. All patients must be screened with TST or IGRA before starting anti-TNF therapy. Hepatitis B (not C) also requires screening but TB is the most critical concern.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','easy','mcq','A 50-year-old man presents with sudden-onset severe pain, redness, and swelling of the first metatarsophalangeal joint at 3 AM. Joint aspiration reveals needle-shaped crystals that are negatively birefringent under polarized light. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Pseudogout","B. Septic arthritis","C. Gout (podagra)","D. Reactive arthritis"]','C','The presentation is classic podagra — acute gout affecting the first MTP joint. Monosodium urate (MSU) crystals are needle-shaped and show negative birefringence (yellow when parallel to the compensator axis). Pseudogout shows rhomboid-shaped CPPD crystals with positive birefringence. Septic arthritis has WBCs without crystals. Reactive arthritis follows infection at another site.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','medium','mcq','A patient with gout is found to have deposits of chalky white material in the ear cartilage and around the MTP joint. Histological examination shows amorphous material surrounded by foreign-body giant cells and fibrous tissue. What are these deposits called?','["A. Rheumatoid nodules","B. Tophi","C. Calcinosis cutis","D. Xanthomas"]','B','Tophi are deposits of monosodium urate crystals that form in chronic tophaceous gout. They appear as chalky white nodules in cartilage (ear helix), tendons, and periarticular tissue. Histologically, they show amorphous urate surrounded by a foreign-body giant cell reaction. They differ from rheumatoid nodules (fibrinoid necrosis + palisading macrophages), calcinosis cutis (calcium phosphate), and xanthomas (lipid-laden macrophages).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','medium','mcq','An 8-year-old boy with intellectual disability, self-mutilation, and choreoathetosis is found to have gout. Which enzyme deficiency is responsible?','["A. Adenosine deaminase deficiency","B. HGPRT (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase) deficiency","C. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","D. Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency"]','D','Lesch-Nyhan syndrome results from complete HGPRT deficiency, an X-linked recessive condition. HGPRT normally salvages hypoxanthine and guanine back to IMP and GMP; its absence forces purines through de novo synthesis with excess uric acid production. Features include gout, nephrolithiasis, intellectual disability, choreoathetosis, and self-mutilation. ADA deficiency causes SCID; G6PD causes hemolytic anemia; PNP deficiency causes immunodeficiency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','hard','mcq','A patient with gout is prescribed allopurinol for long-term urate-lowering therapy. What is the mechanism of action of allopurinol?','["A. Increases renal excretion of uric acid by blocking URAT1 transporter","B. Inhibits xanthine oxidase, reducing conversion of hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid","C. Inhibits tubulin polymerization, preventing neutrophil migration into joints","D. Blocks IL-1 receptor, reducing acute joint inflammation"]','B','Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor. Xanthine oxidase catalyzes the conversion of hypoxanthine to xanthine and then to uric acid. By inhibiting this enzyme, allopurinol reduces uric acid production. Probenecid/lesinurad block URAT1 (uricosuric agents). Colchicine inhibits tubulin polymerization to prevent neutrophil migration. Canakinumab blocks IL-1 for acute gout.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','medium','mcq','An elderly woman is found to have rhomboid-shaped crystals with positive birefringence in her knee joint fluid. X-ray of the knee shows calcification of the fibrocartilage (meniscus). What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Gout","B. Calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (pseudogout)","C. Hydroxyapatite crystal disease","D. Oxalate crystal arthropathy"]','A','CPPD (calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease or pseudogout) is characterized by rhomboid-shaped crystals that are positively birefringent (blue when parallel to the compensator). Chondrocalcinosis (calcification of fibrocartilage such as the meniscus) is a key radiographic finding. It commonly affects the elderly and the knee. Gout has needle-shaped negatively birefringent MSU crystals. Hydroxyapatite crystals are not birefringent.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','easy','mcq','A patient with gout has an acute attack triggered by starting hydrochlorothiazide. What is the mechanism by which thiazide diuretics precipitate gout?','["A. They increase xanthine oxidase activity, raising uric acid production","B. They cause volume depletion, reducing renal urate excretion and raising serum urate","C. They directly precipitate urate crystals in joint spaces","D. They activate NLRP3 inflammasome in macrophages"]','D','Thiazide diuretics cause volume depletion, leading to enhanced proximal tubular reabsorption of urate (along with sodium and water), thereby reducing renal uric acid excretion and raising serum urate levels. This is the mechanism by which thiazides precipitate gout. They do not increase xanthine oxidase activity, directly precipitate crystals, or activate the inflammasome directly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','hard','mcq','Monosodium urate crystals in gout activate which intracellular signaling complex, leading to IL-1beta release and neutrophil recruitment?','["A. TLR4/NF-kB pathway","B. JAK-STAT3 pathway","C. NLRP3 inflammasome","D. MAPK/ERK pathway"]','C','MSU crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome — a multiprotein complex in macrophages that processes pro-IL-1beta to active IL-1beta via caspase-1. IL-1beta is the key cytokine driving the acute neutrophilic inflammatory response in gout. This is why IL-1 inhibitors (anakinra, canakinumab) are effective for refractory acute gout. TLR4/NF-kB mediates LPS responses. JAK-STAT and MAPK have lesser roles in crystal-induced inflammation.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','medium','mcq','A patient develops an acute gout attack 3 days after cardiac surgery. What is the most likely trigger for this postoperative gout attack?','["A. Contamination of joint space during surgery","B. Rapid cell turnover and nucleic acid release from surgical trauma causing purine load","C. Anesthesia directly precipitating urate crystals","D. Postoperative antibiotics inhibiting urate excretion"]','A','Acute surgical stress, trauma, and rapid cell turnover increase purine catabolism and uric acid production. Additionally, postoperative volume shifts, dehydration, and organ stress reduce renal urate excretion. Together these raise serum urate acutely and can precipitate gout attacks. The surgery does not contaminate joints; anesthesia does not directly precipitate crystals; antibiotics generally do not affect urate excretion significantly.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','easy','mcq','What is the mechanism of action of colchicine in treating acute gout?','["A. It inhibits xanthine oxidase to reduce uric acid production","B. It blocks COX-1 and COX-2, reducing prostaglandin synthesis","C. It inhibits tubulin polymerization, impairing neutrophil motility and degranulation","D. It antagonizes the IL-1 receptor on synoviocytes"]','C','Colchicine inhibits tubulin polymerization, which disrupts the cytoskeleton needed for neutrophil chemotaxis, degranulation, and phagocytosis of urate crystals. This blunts the acute inflammatory response without lowering uric acid levels. It is most effective when given within 24 hours of an attack. Xanthine oxidase inhibition is allopurinol''s mechanism. COX inhibition is NSAIDs'' mechanism. IL-1 receptor blockade is anakinra''s mechanism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Gout & Pseudogout','medium','mcq','A patient with gout develops nephrolithiasis. What type of kidney stones are most commonly associated with gout?','["A. Calcium oxalate stones","B. Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones","C. Uric acid stones","D. Calcium phosphate (brushite) stones"]','B','Uric acid stones form in patients with gout due to hyperuricosuria and acidic urine (low pH). Uric acid is poorly soluble in acidic urine and precipitates to form radiolucent stones (not visible on plain x-ray). Calcium oxalate stones are the most common kidney stones overall but are not specifically associated with gout. Struvite stones are associated with urease-producing bacterial infections.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','medium','mcq','A 62-year-old postmenopausal woman sustains a vertebral compression fracture after minimal trauma. DEXA scan shows a T-score of -2.8. Which pathological process best describes osteoporosis?','["A. Decreased bone mass with abnormal mineralization of osteoid","B. Increased osteoclast activity relative to osteoblast activity, with normal bone mineralization","C. Replacement of bone marrow by fibrous tissue with woven bone formation","D. Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals weakening trabecular bone"]','C','Osteoporosis is defined as decreased bone mass with normal mineralization (normal ratio of mineral to osteoid matrix). There is an imbalance between osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic formation, with net bone loss. This distinguishes it from osteomalacia (decreased mineralization with excess osteoid) and Paget''s disease (abnormal remodeling with woven bone). DEXA T-score <= -2.5 defines osteoporosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','hard','mcq','A 75-year-old man develops a hip fracture from a low-energy fall. His DEXA shows T-score of -3.0 affecting both cortical and trabecular bone. Which type of osteoporosis does he have and what is the primary mechanism?','["A. Type I (postmenopausal) osteoporosis due to estrogen deficiency accelerating trabecular bone loss","B. Type II (senile) osteoporosis due to age-related decline in osteoblast function affecting cortical and trabecular bone","C. Secondary osteoporosis due to primary hyperparathyroidism","D. Disuse osteoporosis from immobilization affecting cortical bone only"]','D','Type II (senile) osteoporosis affects both cortical and trabecular bone and occurs in patients >70 years old of both sexes. It results from age-related decline in osteoblast function, decreased calcium absorption, secondary hyperparathyroidism from vitamin D deficiency, and reduced physical activity. Type I (postmenopausal) primarily affects trabecular bone in women aged 50-70 due to estrogen loss. This patient''s age, sex, and pattern fit Type II.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','easy','mcq','Which fracture site in osteoporosis is associated with the highest morbidity and mortality?','["A. Vertebral compression fracture","B. Colles'' fracture of the distal radius","C. Femoral neck (hip) fracture","D. Metatarsal stress fracture"]','A','Hip (femoral neck) fractures are the most serious complication of osteoporosis, with 1-year mortality of 20-30% due to complications including DVT, pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, and functional decline. Vertebral fractures are the most common osteoporotic fracture but have lower immediate mortality. Colles'' fractures are common but rarely fatal. Metatarsal stress fractures are relatively minor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old woman on long-term corticosteroid therapy for asthma develops osteoporosis. Which mechanism is primarily responsible for steroid-induced osteoporosis?','["A. Corticosteroids activate osteoclasts via direct receptor binding","B. Corticosteroids inhibit osteoblast differentiation and function, reducing bone formation","C. Corticosteroids increase calcitonin, leading to excessive bone resorption","D. Corticosteroids promote calcium deposition in soft tissues, depleting bone calcium"]','B','Glucocorticoids cause osteoporosis primarily by suppressing osteoblast differentiation and promoting osteoblast apoptosis (reducing bone formation), while also increasing RANKL expression and suppressing OPG (increasing osteoclast activity). They also reduce intestinal calcium absorption and increase renal calcium excretion. This is the most common cause of secondary osteoporosis. Calcitonin inhibits (not increases) osteoclasts.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','hard','mcq','Bisphosphonates are first-line treatment for osteoporosis. What is their mechanism of action at the cellular level?','["A. They bind to calcium in hydroxyapatite and are internalized by osteoclasts, inhibiting farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase and disrupting osteoclast function","B. They stimulate estrogen receptors on osteoblasts, increasing bone formation","C. They bind RANKL, preventing it from activating osteoclast precursors","D. They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in osteoclasts, impairing acid secretion"]','D','Bisphosphonates (e.g., alendronate, zoledronate) bind to hydroxyapatite in bone and are ingested by osteoclasts during resorption. Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (an enzyme in the mevalonate pathway), preventing prenylation of small GTP-binding proteins needed for osteoclast cytoskeletal function, leading to osteoclast apoptosis. RANKL is blocked by denosumab. Estrogen receptor agonism is the mechanism of raloxifene.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','medium','mcq','A woman sustains a Colles'' fracture at age 53. Which risk factor most likely predisposes her to this fracture at this age?','["A. Male sex and advanced age","B. Estrogen deficiency following menopause causing type I osteoporosis","C. Chronic alcohol use causing hepatic osteodystrophy","D. Hyperparathyroidism causing subperiosteal bone resorption"]','C','Colles'' fracture (distal radius fracture) is a classic presentation of type I (postmenopausal) osteoporosis in women aged 50-70. Estrogen deficiency after menopause accelerates trabecular bone loss, and the distal radius is rich in trabecular bone. Male sex and advanced age suggest type II senile osteoporosis. Alcohol and hyperparathyroidism can cause secondary osteoporosis but are less common causes in a 53-year-old woman.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','easy','mcq','A drug company develops a monoclonal antibody that binds RANKL, preventing it from activating osteoclasts. This drug (denosumab) treats osteoporosis. What does RANKL normally do?','["A. It stimulates osteoblast differentiation and bone matrix synthesis","B. It binds RANK on osteoclast precursors, promoting osteoclast differentiation and activation","C. It inhibits PTH secretion from the parathyroid glands","D. It promotes calcium absorption in the small intestine"]','A','RANKL (Receptor Activator of NF-kB Ligand) is expressed by osteoblasts and stromal cells. It binds to RANK on osteoclast precursors, triggering their differentiation into mature osteoclasts and activating bone resorption. OPG (osteoprotegerin) is a decoy receptor that inhibits RANKL. Denosumab mimics OPG by binding RANKL. RANKL does not stimulate osteoblasts, inhibit PTH, or affect intestinal calcium absorption.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','medium','mcq','Which imaging modality and measurement threshold is used to diagnose osteoporosis?','["A. Plain X-ray; 30% reduction in bone density","B. CT scan; Hounsfield units <100","C. DEXA scan; T-score of -2.5 or below","D. MRI; reduction in marrow fat signal"]','B','The WHO defines osteoporosis by DEXA (dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry) with a T-score of -2.5 or below (2.5 standard deviations below the young adult mean peak bone mass). T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 is osteopenia. Plain X-rays can suggest osteoporosis but require >30% bone loss to be apparent and are not diagnostic. CT and MRI are not standard diagnostic tools for osteoporosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','hard','mcq','A patient with osteoporosis has multiple thoracic vertebral compression fractures causing progressive kyphosis ("dowager''s hump"). Why are vertebral compression fractures more common than fractures of other sites in osteoporosis?','["A. Vertebral bodies consist predominantly of trabecular bone, which is more metabolically active and loses mass faster than cortical bone","B. Vertebral bodies have thinner cortical shells due to their embryological origin","C. The spine receives less mechanical loading than limb bones, causing preferential bone loss","D. Vertebral bodies lack a periosteum to support bone remodeling"]','A','Vertebral bodies are composed predominantly of trabecular (cancellous) bone, which has much greater surface area than cortical bone and is therefore more metabolically active. In type I (postmenopausal) osteoporosis driven by estrogen deficiency, trabecular bone is resorbed preferentially, leading to vertebral compression fractures as the most common site. The increased metabolic activity means trabecular bone responds faster to hormonal changes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Osteoporosis','medium','mcq','Which of the following is NOT a recognized risk factor for primary osteoporosis?','["A. Cigarette smoking","B. Obesity","C. Low dietary calcium intake","D. Family history of osteoporosis"]','D','Obesity is actually protective against osteoporosis, as excess body weight provides mechanical loading that stimulates bone formation, and adipose tissue produces estrogen via aromatization of androgens. Cigarette smoking, low dietary calcium, sedentary lifestyle, alcohol excess, early menopause, and family history are all risk factors for osteoporosis. The mechanical loading from obesity promotes higher bone mineral density.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old woman presents with malar rash, photosensitivity, arthralgia, and proteinuria. ANA is positive at 1:640. Which antibody is most specific for SLE and also correlates with disease activity?','["A. Anti-Sm (anti-Smith)","B. Anti-dsDNA","C. Anti-Ro (SS-A)","D. Anti-histone"]','A','Anti-dsDNA antibodies are the most specific marker that also correlates with disease activity — rising titers signal flares, and levels fall with treatment. Anti-Smith antibodies are highly specific for SLE but do not fluctuate with disease activity. Anti-Ro is associated with Sjogren''s and neonatal lupus. Anti-histone antibodies are associated with drug-induced lupus. ANA is the most sensitive test for SLE but not specific.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','hard','mcq','A patient with SLE undergoes renal biopsy showing diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis with "wire-loop" capillary changes on light microscopy, granular IgG and complement deposits on immunofluorescence, and subendothelial electron-dense deposits on electron microscopy. What is the WHO/ISN class and prognosis?','["A. Class II mesangial lupus nephritis — excellent prognosis","B. Class III focal lupus nephritis — moderate prognosis","C. Class V membranous lupus nephritis — nephrotic syndrome","D. Class IV diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis — worst prognosis"]','D','Class IV diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis is the most common and most severe form of lupus nephritis. Wire-loop lesions represent massive subendothelial immune complex deposits thickening the GBM. It presents with nephritic syndrome (hematuria, proteinuria, HTN, declining GFR) and has the worst renal prognosis without aggressive immunosuppression (cyclophosphamide + steroids). Class II is mild mesangial; Class III involves <50% of glomeruli; Class V is membranous.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','easy','mcq','A patient with SLE has a false-positive VDRL test. Which antibody is responsible for this serologic finding?','["A. Anti-dsDNA antibody","B. Antiphospholipid antibody","C. Anti-Smith antibody","D. Rheumatoid factor"]','C','Antiphospholipid antibodies (lupus anticoagulant, anti-cardiolipin, anti-beta2-glycoprotein I) cross-react with cardiolipin in the VDRL/RPR tests for syphilis, causing a false-positive result. These antibodies paradoxically cause a prolonged aPTT in vitro but thrombosis in vivo. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are specific for SLE but do not cause false-positive VDRL. RF causes false-positive tests for other conditions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','medium','mcq','Echocardiography in a patient with SLE reveals small, sterile verrucous vegetations on both surfaces of the mitral valve. What is this lesion called?','["A. Marantic endocarditis (non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis)","B. Rheumatic endocarditis (MacCallum patch)","C. Libman-Sacks endocarditis","D. Infective endocarditis"]','D','Libman-Sacks endocarditis is the characteristic cardiac lesion of SLE, consisting of small (1-4 mm), sterile, verrucous vegetations that occur on both surfaces (atrial and ventricular) of valve leaflets, most commonly the mitral valve. Unlike infective endocarditis (large, destructive on valve tips) and rheumatic endocarditis (along line of closure), Libman-Sacks vegetations are sterile immune complex deposits. Marantic endocarditis occurs in wasting states.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','medium','mcq','A SLE patient is found to have low C3 and C4 complement levels during a disease flare. What is the mechanism of complement consumption in SLE?','["A. SLE suppresses hepatic synthesis of complement proteins","B. Immune complexes of auto-antibodies with self-antigens activate the classical complement pathway, consuming C3 and C4","C. Anti-complement antibodies directly neutralize C3 and C4","D. Renal losses of complement proteins through the glomerulus in nephrotic syndrome"]','A','In active SLE, immune complexes (e.g., anti-dsDNA bound to DNA) deposit in tissues and activate the classical complement pathway (C1q → C4 → C2 → C3), leading to consumption of C3 and C4. Low complement levels correlate with disease activity, particularly lupus nephritis. Complement levels rise with remission. SLE does not suppress hepatic synthesis. Anti-complement antibodies are not the mechanism. Renal protein loss does not selectively deplete C3/C4.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','hard','mcq','Which HLA haplotypes are associated with increased susceptibility to SLE, and what is the proposed mechanism?','["A. HLA-B27 and B8; abnormal antigen presentation of nuclear antigens","B. HLA-DR2 and DR3; associated with complement deficiencies (C2, C4) and impaired immune complex clearance","C. HLA-DR4 and DR1; presentation of citrullinated peptides to CD4+ T cells","D. HLA-Cw6 and B13; abnormal keratinocyte antigen expression"]','B','HLA-DR2 and HLA-DR3 haplotypes are associated with SLE. HLA-DR3 is in linkage disequilibrium with C4A null alleles (C4AQ0) and C2 deficiency, impairing immune complex clearance and predisposing to SLE. Impaired complement-mediated clearance of apoptotic debris allows self-antigens to accumulate and trigger autoimmunity. HLA-DR4 is associated with RA. HLA-B27 with spondyloarthropathies. HLA-Cw6 with psoriasis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman with SLE develops recurrent arterial and venous thromboses and two miscarriages. Tests reveal prolonged aPTT that does not correct with normal plasma, and positive anti-cardiolipin antibodies. What syndrome does she have?','["A. Disseminated intravascular coagulation","B. Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome","C. Protein C deficiency","D. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura"]','C','Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APS) is characterized by recurrent arterial/venous thromboses and pregnancy losses (due to placental thrombosis) in the presence of antiphospholipid antibodies (lupus anticoagulant, anti-cardiolipin, anti-beta2-GPI). Paradoxically, the lupus anticoagulant prolongs aPTT in vitro (inhibits phospholipid-dependent clotting) but causes thrombosis in vivo by activating endothelium and platelets. APS can occur as primary or secondary to SLE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','easy','mcq','Which serologic test is the most sensitive screening test for SLE, present in >95% of patients?','["A. Anti-dsDNA","B. Anti-Smith","C. Antinuclear antibody (ANA)","D. Anti-CCP"]','D','ANA (antinuclear antibody) is the most sensitive test for SLE, present in >95% of patients. However, it is not specific (positive in many other autoimmune diseases, infections, and even normal individuals at low titer). Anti-dsDNA is specific but less sensitive (~70%). Anti-Smith is highly specific (>99%) but only ~25% sensitive. Anti-CCP is specific for RA. A positive ANA should be followed by more specific tests.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','hard','mcq','A patient taking procainamide for arrhythmia develops arthritis, serositis, and a positive ANA with anti-histone antibodies. The anti-dsDNA test is negative. What is the diagnosis and what happens when the drug is stopped?','["A. Drug-induced lupus; symptoms and antibodies resolve within weeks to months of stopping the drug","B. SLE triggered by procainamide; symptoms persist permanently","C. Serum sickness from procainamide metabolites; urticaria dominates the picture","D. Pseudo-SLE requiring long-term immunosuppression"]','A','Drug-induced lupus (DIL) is associated with procainamide, hydralazine, isoniazid, and others. It is characterized by ANA positivity, anti-histone antibodies, and symptoms of arthritis/serositis/fever, but notably lacks the anti-dsDNA antibodies and renal/CNS involvement typical of idiopathic SLE. When the offending drug is discontinued, symptoms and antibodies gradually resolve over weeks to months. Long-term immunosuppression is not required.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','medium','mcq','Which skin lesion in SLE causes permanent scarring and hair loss, and is NOT associated with systemic disease activity?','["A. Malar (butterfly) rash","B. Photosensitive maculopapular rash","C. Discoid lupus erythematosus","D. Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus"]','B','Discoid lupus erythematosus (DLE) consists of chronic, coin-shaped plaques typically on the face/scalp that heal with permanent scarring, depigmentation, and scarring alopecia. DLE can occur alone (isolated skin disease) or as part of systemic SLE, but its activity does not correlate with systemic disease severity. The malar rash is transient and associated with flares. Subacute cutaneous lupus heals without scarring and is associated with anti-Ro.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','medium','mcq','A 24-year-old male presents with 3 years of inflammatory back pain, worse in the morning and improving with exercise. Pelvic X-ray shows blurring and sclerosis of the sacroiliac joints bilaterally. HLA-B27 is positive. What is the earliest radiographic sign of ankylosing spondylitis?','["A. Bamboo spine (syndesmophyte bridging)","B. Erosion of vertebral body corners (Romanus lesion)","C. Sacroiliitis","D. Facet joint fusion"]','C','Sacroiliitis is the earliest and most characteristic radiographic finding in ankylosing spondylitis, typically bilateral and symmetric, showing joint space irregularity, sclerosis, and eventually fusion. Bamboo spine (syndesmophytes bridging vertebrae) is a late finding. Romanus lesions (vertebral corner erosions) are early but appear after sacroiliitis. HLA-B27 is present in >90% of AS patients but is not diagnostic alone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','hard','mcq','Which extra-articular manifestation of ankylosing spondylitis is most common and can lead to blindness if untreated?','["A. Aortic regurgitation from aortic root dilation","B. Anterior uveitis (iritis)","C. Pulmonary fibrosis of upper lobes","D. IgA nephropathy"]','D','Anterior uveitis (iritis) is the most common extra-articular manifestation of ankylosing spondylitis, occurring in ~25-40% of patients. It presents with acute onset of eye pain, photophobia, and redness (not gradual visual loss like posterior uveitis). If untreated, it can cause synechiae, glaucoma, and blindness. Aortic regurgitation, upper lobe fibrosis, and IgA nephropathy also occur but are less common than uveitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','easy','mcq','A radiograph of the lumbar spine in a patient with advanced ankylosing spondylitis shows bridging ossification of the outer fibers of the annulus fibrosus between vertebral bodies, creating a "bamboo" appearance. What are these ossifications called?','["A. Osteophytes","B. Syndesmophytes","C. Enthesophytes","D. Chondrophytes"]','A','Syndesmophytes are vertical ossifications of the outer annulus fibrosus fibers (Sharpey''s fibers) in ankylosing spondylitis, bridging adjacent vertebral bodies to create the "bamboo spine" appearance. They differ from osteophytes (horizontal bony outgrowths at disc margins in OA) and enthesophytes (ossification at tendon/ligament insertions). This is a late radiographic finding indicating disease progression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','medium','mcq','Which of the following best describes enthesitis, a hallmark feature of ankylosing spondylitis and other seronegative spondyloarthropathies?','["A. Inflammation within synovial joints causing effusion and pannus","B. Inflammation at the insertion of ligaments, tendons, and joint capsules into bone","C. Inflammation of the intervertebral disc (discitis)","D. Inflammation of the vertebral end plates causing Modic changes"]','B','Enthesitis is inflammation at the enthesis — the site where tendons, ligaments, joint capsules, and fascia attach to bone. In AS, the Achilles tendon insertion, plantar fascia, and spinal ligament insertions are characteristically involved. This leads to new bone formation (enthesophytes) as part of the healing process. Synovial inflammation and pannus are more characteristic of RA. Discitis involves the disc itself; Modic changes are end-plate degenerative changes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis has failed NSAIDs. Which biologic therapy is recommended as second-line treatment?','["A. Methotrexate","B. Hydroxychloroquine","C. TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., etanercept or adalimumab)","D. Rituximab (anti-CD20)"]','D','TNF-alpha inhibitors (etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab) are the recommended biologic therapy for AS patients who fail NSAIDs. They significantly reduce inflammation, improve symptoms, and slow radiographic progression. IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab) are an alternative. Methotrexate has limited efficacy for axial AS (works for peripheral arthritis). Hydroxychloroquine and rituximab are not effective for AS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','hard','mcq','The Schober test is used to assess lumbar spine mobility in ankylosing spondylitis. A patient marks a point 5 cm below and 10 cm above the lumbosacral junction in standing position. On forward flexion, the distance increases from 15 cm to only 17 cm. What does this suggest?','["A. Normal lumbar spine mobility — an increase of 2 cm is expected","B. Reduced lumbar flexion consistent with AS — normal increase is ≥5 cm","C. Sacroiliac joint fusion — this test specifically measures SI joint mobility","D. Thoracic kyphosis — the test measures thoracic rather than lumbar mobility"]','B','The modified Schober test measures lumbar spine flexion. With marks 5 cm below and 10 cm above the L5/S1 junction (total 15 cm), normal forward flexion increases this distance by ≥5 cm (to ≥20 cm). An increase of only 2 cm (to 17 cm) indicates significantly reduced lumbar flexion, consistent with ankylosing spondylitis causing spinal rigidity. The test assesses lumbar, not thoracic or SI joint, mobility.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','medium','mcq','Which HLA allele is present in >90% of patients with ankylosing spondylitis?','["A. HLA-DR4","B. HLA-Cw6","C. HLA-B27","D. HLA-DR2"]','C','HLA-B27 is present in >90% of patients with ankylosing spondylitis. The general population prevalence of HLA-B27 varies by ethnicity (8% in Caucasians), but among AS patients it approaches 90-95%. While HLA-B27 is the strongest genetic risk factor, it is not sufficient alone — only ~5% of HLA-B27 positive individuals develop AS. HLA-DR4 is associated with RA; HLA-Cw6 with psoriasis; HLA-DR2/DR3 with SLE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','easy','mcq','Which demographic group is most commonly affected by ankylosing spondylitis?','["A. Elderly women over 65 years","B. Young males under 40 years","C. Postmenopausal women aged 50-65","D. Children aged 5-15 years"]','A','Ankylosing spondylitis predominantly affects young males (male:female ratio approximately 3:1) with onset typically before age 40, often in the late teens to early 30s. The combination of HLA-B27, young age, male sex, and inflammatory back pain (improving with exercise, not rest) is the classic presentation. Elderly women are more prone to osteoporosis. Children may develop enthesitis-related arthritis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','hard','mcq','A patient with ankylosing spondylitis develops reduced chest expansion of 2 cm (normal >5 cm). What is the pathological basis of reduced chest expansion in AS?','["A. Costovertebral and costosternal joint involvement leading to fusion of the thoracic cage","B. Upper lobe pulmonary fibrosis restricting chest wall movement","C. Pericardial thickening from serositis limiting cardiac expansion","D. Diaphragmatic paralysis from C3/C4 nerve root compression"]','C','Ankylosing spondylitis involves enthesitis and ossification at costovertebral and costosternal joints, progressively fusing the thoracic cage. This restricts chest wall expansion, reducing respiratory reserve. Patients compensate with diaphragmatic breathing. Upper lobe pulmonary fibrosis is a late extra-articular complication of AS (apical fibrosis) but does not directly cause reduced chest expansion. Pericardial and diaphragmatic involvement are not the primary mechanism.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Ankylosing Spondylitis','medium','mcq','A patient with ankylosing spondylitis has markedly elevated ESR and CRP. These acute phase reactants indicate active disease. Which statement best characterizes the inflammatory back pain of AS compared to mechanical back pain?','["A. AS back pain is worst with activity and relieved by rest","B. AS back pain is worse in the morning, associated with prolonged morning stiffness, and improves with exercise and NSAIDs","C. AS back pain radiates to the posterior thigh consistent with nerve root compression","D. AS back pain is associated with muscle spasm that responds to muscle relaxants"]','D','Inflammatory back pain in AS is characteristically worse in the morning (prolonged stiffness lasting >45 minutes), improves with exercise/activity (not rest), and responds to NSAIDs. This distinguishes it from mechanical back pain (worse with activity, relieved by rest). The inflammatory nature is supported by elevated ESR/CRP. Morning stiffness that improves with activity is a key diagnostic clue.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','easy','mcq','A 35-year-old man with known plaque psoriasis develops painful swelling of multiple fingers with a "sausage" appearance. X-ray shows erosive changes at the DIP joints. What is the most characteristic joint pattern of psoriatic arthritis?','["A. Symmetric proximal interphalangeal and metacarpophalangeal joint involvement","B. Distal interphalangeal joint involvement with dactylitis","C. Sacroiliac joint involvement exclusively","D. Large joint oligoarthritis of knees and ankles only"]','B','Distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint involvement is the most characteristic pattern of psoriatic arthritis and virtually unique among inflammatory arthropathies. Dactylitis ("sausage digit") from diffuse tenosynovitis of an entire digit is also characteristic. This differs from RA, which typically spares DIP joints and involves PIP/MCP joints symmetrically. Psoriatic arthritis is seronegative (RF negative).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','medium','mcq','An X-ray of a patient with severe psoriatic arthritis shows the distal end of one phalanx eroded and tapered into a pencil-like shape fitting into the cup-shaped base of the next phalanx. What is this finding called?','["A. Bamboo spine","B. Pencil-in-cup deformity","C. Erosive arthritis with osteophytes","D. Rugger jersey spine"]','B','The pencil-in-cup deformity is a radiographic hallmark of severe erosive psoriatic arthritis, occurring most commonly at DIP joints. Osteolysis of the distal end of the proximal phalanx creates a pencil-like point, while erosion of the proximal end of the distal phalanx creates a cup — resulting in the characteristic pencil-in-cup appearance. This distinguishes severe PsA from RA and OA radiographically.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','medium','mcq','Which nail findings in a patient with psoriasis should raise suspicion for psoriatic arthritis?','["A. Koilonychia (spoon-shaped nails) and leukonychia","B. Nail pitting, onycholysis, and subungual hyperkeratosis","C. Beau''s lines and Terry''s nails","D. Mees'' lines and nail clubbing"]','D','Nail pitting (punctate depressions from psoriasis affecting the nail matrix), onycholysis (separation of nail plate from the nail bed), and subungual hyperkeratosis are the characteristic nail changes in psoriasis that predict psoriatic arthritis development. DIP joint involvement and nail disease often co-occur as the extensor tendon inserts near the nail matrix. Koilonychia suggests iron deficiency; Beau''s lines indicate systemic illness; Mees'' lines suggest arsenic poisoning.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','hard','mcq','A 40-year-old woman with psoriatic arthritis is RF-negative and anti-CCP-negative. Which HLA antigens are associated with psoriatic arthritis?','["A. HLA-B27 (axial disease) and HLA-Cw6 (associated with psoriasis)","B. HLA-DR4 and HLA-DR1 (shared epitope)","C. HLA-B8 and HLA-DR3 (complement deficiency haplotype)","D. HLA-DR2 and HLA-DQ1 (linked to anti-dsDNA production)"]','A','HLA-B27 is associated with the axial (spinal) form of psoriatic arthritis and sacroiliitis, similar to other seronegative spondyloarthropathies. HLA-Cw6 is the primary HLA association with psoriasis itself and confers the highest risk for developing psoriatic skin disease. Psoriatic arthritis is seronegative (RF and anti-CCP negative), distinguishing it from RA (HLA-DR4). HLA-DR2/DR3 is associated with SLE.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','easy','mcq','Which of the following best characterizes the distribution of psoriatic arthritis compared to rheumatoid arthritis?','["A. Psoriatic arthritis is symmetric and involves MCP joints; RA is asymmetric","B. Psoriatic arthritis is often asymmetric and seronegative; RA is symmetric and seropositive","C. Psoriatic arthritis involves large joints only; RA involves small joints only","D. Both conditions exclusively affect joints of the hands and feet"]','B','Psoriatic arthritis is characteristically asymmetric (in most patterns), seronegative (RF and anti-CCP negative), involves DIP joints, and has dactylitis/enthesitis. Rheumatoid arthritis is characteristically symmetric, involves MCP and PIP joints (sparing DIP), and is often seropositive (RF and/or anti-CCP positive). Both can involve small and large joints. The asymmetry and DIP involvement are key distinguishing features of PsA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','medium','mcq','What is dactylitis and in which conditions is it characteristically found?','["A. Inflammation of the MCP joints causing knuckle swelling; seen in gout and pseudogout","B. Diffuse swelling of an entire digit from tenosynovitis; seen in psoriatic arthritis and reactive arthritis","C. Osteomyelitis of phalangeal bones causing digital necrosis; seen in sickle cell disease","D. Deposition of calcium hydroxyapatite crystals in finger tendons; seen in renal failure"]','D','Dactylitis (''sausage digit'') is diffuse swelling of an entire finger or toe due to tenosynovitis (inflammation of the tendon sheath) combined with synovitis of the adjacent joints. It is characteristic of psoriatic arthritis and reactive arthritis (and also seen in sickle cell disease). The entire digit, including the soft tissues between joints, is uniformly swollen. It differs from gout (acute joint involvement) and osteomyelitis (infection with bone involvement).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','hard','mcq','A patient with psoriatic arthritis develops mutilans arthritis — severe joint destruction with telescoping of digits. What pathological process underlies arthritis mutilans?','["A. Massive synovial pannus causing joint destruction and osteolysis of phalangeal bone","B. Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition causing enzymatic bone dissolution","C. Septic arthritis complicating psoriatic lesions with direct bacterial bone invasion","D. Amyloid deposition from chronic inflammation compressing bone vessels"]','A','Arthritis mutilans is the most destructive pattern of psoriatic arthritis, characterized by severe osteolysis of phalangeal bones leading to ''telescoping'' or ''opera-glass'' digits. The underlying process is aggressive pannus formation (similar to RA) causing extensive bone resorption and cartilage destruction at DIP and PIP joints. The bone is so eroded that digits can be compressed and re-extended like a telescope. It occurs in ~5% of psoriatic arthritis patients.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','medium','mcq','Which pattern of psoriatic arthritis most closely resembles rheumatoid arthritis in its distribution?','["A. DIP joint predominant pattern","B. Asymmetric oligoarthritis","C. Symmetric polyarthritis affecting PIP and MCP joints","D. Arthritis mutilans"]','C','The symmetric polyarthritis pattern of psoriatic arthritis (affecting ~15-20% of PsA patients) closely resembles RA in its symmetric involvement of PIP and MCP joints, making it difficult to distinguish clinically. However, PsA remains seronegative (negative RF and anti-CCP), has DIP involvement, and associated psoriatic skin/nail disease. Anti-CCP negativity and skin findings help differentiate from RA.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','easy','mcq','What proportion of patients with psoriasis develop psoriatic arthritis?','["A. Less than 1%","B. Approximately 5-10%","C. Approximately 20-30%","D. Greater than 50%"]','A','Approximately 20-30% of patients with psoriasis (plaque psoriasis) develop psoriatic arthritis. Nail involvement (pitting, onycholysis) is the strongest predictor of arthritis development among psoriasis patients. The arthritis can precede, coincide with, or (most commonly) follow the skin disease. Scalp psoriasis and inverse psoriasis are also risk factors for arthritis development.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Psoriatic Arthritis','medium','mcq','A patient with psoriatic arthritis has predominantly axial involvement with sacroiliitis and spondylitis. How does the spondylitis pattern in PsA differ from ankylosing spondylitis radiographically?','["A. PsA spondylitis is always bilateral and symmetric like AS","B. PsA spondylitis may be asymmetric with ''skip'' lesions and non-marginal (parasyndesmophyte) ossifications","C. PsA spondylitis exclusively involves the cervical spine","D. PsA spondylitis shows calcification of anterior longitudinal ligament only"]','B','The spondylitis of psoriatic arthritis differs from ankylosing spondylitis in that it tends to be asymmetric, can have "skip" lesions, and may show non-marginal (bulky, paravertebral) ossifications (parasyndesmophytes) rather than the delicate marginal syndesmophytes of AS. The sacroiliitis in PsA may also be asymmetric (AS is characteristically bilateral and symmetric). Both are HLA-B27-associated.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','medium','mcq','A 15-year-old boy presents with knee pain and swelling. X-ray shows a destructive lesion in the distal femur metaphysis with periosteal elevation forming a triangular density at the edge of the tumor (Codman''s triangle) and a "sunburst" pattern of periosteal new bone. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Ewing sarcoma","B. Giant cell tumor of bone","C. Osteosarcoma","D. Chondrosarcoma"]','C','Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor. It characteristically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones (distal femur and proximal tibia most common) in adolescents and young adults. Radiographic findings include Codman''s triangle (periosteal elevation at tumor margin) and sunburst pattern (tumor bone radiating outward). Ewing sarcoma shows onion-skin periosteal reaction in the diaphysis. Giant cell tumor occurs at the epiphysis in adults.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','hard','mcq','A 12-year-old presents with a diaphyseal bone tumor. Biopsy shows sheets of small, round, blue cells with scant cytoplasm. Cytogenetics reveals t(11;22)(q24;q12) creating EWSR1-FLI1 fusion. What is the diagnosis and what periosteal reaction is characteristic?','["A. Osteosarcoma; Codman''s triangle","B. Ewing sarcoma; onion-skin periosteal reaction","C. Rhabdomyosarcoma; no periosteal reaction","D. Lymphoma of bone; permeative lytic lesion"]','D','Ewing sarcoma is characterized by: (1) children/adolescents 5-15 years, (2) diaphyseal location (unlike osteosarcoma in metaphysis), (3) sheets of small round blue cells with CD99 positivity, (4) t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene, and (5) onion-skin periosteal reaction (multiple layers of reactive periosteal bone). It is highly aggressive but sensitive to chemotherapy and radiation. Osteosarcoma shows sunburst pattern and Codman''s triangle.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman presents with knee pain. X-ray shows an epiphyseal lytic lesion with a "soap-bubble" appearance extending to the articular surface of the distal femur. Biopsy reveals multinucleated osteoclast-type giant cells in a background of mononuclear stromal cells. What is this tumor and how is it classified?','["A. Osteosarcoma — malignant bone-forming tumor","B. Giant cell tumor of bone — locally aggressive but usually benign, may rarely metastasize","C. Chondroblastoma — benign cartilage tumor of epiphysis","D. Aneurysmal bone cyst — benign cyst with blood-filled spaces"]','A','Giant cell tumor of bone (osteoclastoma) typically occurs in patients aged 20-40, at the epiphysis of long bones (distal femur, proximal tibia, distal radius), and extends to the articular surface. The soap-bubble radiographic appearance reflects the expansile lytic pattern. Histologically, multinucleated osteoclast-type giant cells are dispersed among mononuclear stromal cells (which are the neoplastic component). GCT is locally aggressive, may recur, and rarely metastasizes to the lung.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','easy','mcq','An 80-year-old man with Paget''s disease of bone in the pelvis develops rapidly worsening pelvic pain and a soft tissue mass. Biopsy reveals a high-grade sarcoma. What is the most likely sarcomatous transformation?','["A. Chondrosarcoma","B. Fibrosarcoma","C. Osteosarcoma","D. Ewing sarcoma"]','C','Sarcomatous transformation occurs in <1% of Paget''s disease patients but is a serious complication that typically presents in the elderly with rapidly worsening pain and a soft tissue mass. The most common sarcoma arising in Paget''s bone is osteosarcoma (>50%), followed by fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma. The prognosis is very poor with a 5-year survival of ~5-8%. This represents the ''secondary'' form of osteosarcoma (distinct from the primary adolescent form).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','medium','mcq','What does Codman''s triangle represent radiographically in osteosarcoma?','["A. The central lucent zone within the tumor representing necrosis","B. Periosteal elevation at the edge of the tumor where the cortex is lifted, forming a triangular density","C. The triangular region of calcification representing tumor bone matrix","D. A stress fracture pattern through the tumor site"]','A','Codman''s triangle is formed when the growing tumor lifts the periosteum from the cortex at the tumor''s margin, and the periosteum attempts to form new reactive bone at the angle between the elevated periosteum and the cortex. This creates a triangular radiodense region at the edge of the tumor. It indicates aggressive periosteal reaction but is not specific to osteosarcoma — it can occur in any rapidly growing bone tumor including Ewing sarcoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old man presents with a slow-growing, painful mass in the right iliac bone. CT shows a lobulated lesion with rings and arcs of calcification (chondroid matrix). Biopsy shows chondroid tissue with mildly atypical chondrocytes in lacunae with focal myxoid change. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Osteosarcoma — metaphyseal location and age","B. Enchondroma — benign cartilage lesion in flat bone","C. Chondrosarcoma — malignant cartilage tumor common in adults and flat bones","D. Chondroblastoma — epiphyseal cartilage tumor in adolescents"]','D','Chondrosarcoma is the second most common primary malignant bone tumor. It predominantly affects adults and the elderly (age >40) and favors the flat bones (pelvis, ribs, scapula) and proximal long bone metaphyses. Radiographically, rings-and-arcs calcification of chondroid matrix is characteristic. Histologically, mildly atypical chondrocytes in lacunae with myxoid stroma are seen. Enchondroma is benign but can transform to chondrosarcoma in flat bones. It does not respond well to chemotherapy/radiation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','medium','mcq','Osteosarcoma has a bimodal age distribution. Which two age groups are affected and what are the predisposing conditions in the older group?','["A. Ages 5-10 (congenital) and ages 40-50 (hereditary retinoblastoma)","B. Ages 10-20 (primary, no predisposing condition) and older adults with Paget''s disease, prior radiation, or Li-Fraumeni syndrome","C. Ages 20-30 (hormone-driven) and ages 60-70 (vitamin D deficiency)","D. Ages 1-5 (embryonal) and ages 50-60 (chronic osteomyelitis)"]','B','Osteosarcoma has a bimodal incidence: (1) a primary peak in adolescents aged 10-20 (most common primary malignant bone tumor in this group, typically occurring near growth plates in the metaphysis of long bones) with no clear predisposing condition; and (2) a secondary peak in older adults (>60 years) associated with Paget''s disease of bone, prior radiation therapy, or inherited predispositions (Li-Fraumeni syndrome with p53 mutation, hereditary retinoblastoma with RB1 mutation).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','easy','mcq','What is the most common primary malignant bone tumor overall?','["A. Chondrosarcoma","B. Ewing sarcoma","C. Osteosarcoma","D. Giant cell tumor"]','B','Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant tumor of bone (excluding multiple myeloma and lymphoma which are tumors of bone marrow). It arises from primitive mesenchymal bone-forming cells and produces malignant osteoid/bone matrix. Chondrosarcoma is second most common. Ewing sarcoma is third. Giant cell tumor is classified as locally aggressive rather than frankly malignant in most cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','hard','mcq','A teenage patient with osteosarcoma undergoes tumor biopsy. The tumor shows neoplastic cells producing pink (eosinophilic) osteoid matrix. Which histological finding is the key diagnostic criterion for osteosarcoma?','["A. Presence of osteoclast-type multinucleated giant cells","B. Small round blue cells with CD99 positivity","C. Production of malignant osteoid/bone by neoplastic cells","D. Chondroid matrix with rings-and-arcs calcification"]','C','The diagnostic criterion for osteosarcoma is the production of malignant osteoid (immature bone matrix) directly by the neoplastic cells. This pink, amorphous material laid down between tumor cells confirms the diagnosis on biopsy, even without bone formation. Osteoclast-type giant cells suggest giant cell tumor. Small round blue cells with CD99 positivity indicate Ewing sarcoma. Chondroid matrix with rings-and-arcs calcification indicates chondrosarcoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Bone Tumours','medium','mcq','A 10-year-old with Ewing sarcoma of the tibia diaphysis has fever and elevated ESR. The clinical presentation can mimic which infectious condition?','["A. Reactive arthritis","B. Acute osteomyelitis","C. Septic arthritis","D. Rheumatic fever"]','D','Ewing sarcoma can mimic osteomyelitis (acute bone infection) because both present with fever, localized bone pain, elevated inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP, WBC), and a destructive diaphyseal lesion on imaging. The onion-skin periosteal reaction on X-ray can also suggest infection. Biopsy is essential to distinguish between these entities. This diagnostic pitfall is clinically important because delay in diagnosing Ewing sarcoma worsens prognosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old man presents with a soft, mobile, painless subcutaneous mass on his upper back. Excision reveals a well-circumscribed tumor composed entirely of mature adipocytes. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Liposarcoma","B. Lipoma","C. Rhabdomyosarcoma","D. Fibrosarcoma"]','A','Lipoma is the most common benign soft tissue tumor, composed of mature, well-differentiated adipocytes with no cellular atypia. They are typically soft, mobile, painless, and well-circumscribed. Liposarcoma is malignant and contains lipoblasts (immature fat cells with scalloped nuclei indented by cytoplasmic lipid vacuoles). Rhabdomyosarcoma and fibrosarcoma are malignant tumors of muscle and fibrous tissue respectively.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','medium','mcq','A 6-year-old boy presents with a rapidly growing mass in the right orbit causing proptosis. Biopsy shows small round blue cells with strap-like rhabdomyoblasts that are desmin-positive. What is the diagnosis and what is its most common site in children?','["A. Neuroblastoma; adrenal gland","B. Rhabdomyosarcoma; head and neck (including orbit)","C. Ewing sarcoma; bone diaphysis","D. Wilms tumor; kidney"]','B','Rhabdomyosarcoma (RMS) is the most common soft tissue sarcoma in children. It arises from primitive mesenchymal cells showing skeletal muscle differentiation. The most common sites are head and neck (including orbit, nasopharynx) and genitourinary tract. Histologically, embryonal RMS shows small round cells and rhabdomyoblasts (strap cells with cross-striations), positive for desmin and myogenin. It can arise where there is no skeletal muscle (showing its primitive origin).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old patient has a large retroperitoneal mass. Biopsy shows pleomorphic cells with multivacuolated lipoblasts (cells with scalloped nuclei indented by lipid vacuoles). What is the diagnosis?','["A. Lipoma","B. Liposarcoma","C. Leiomyosarcoma","D. Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor"]','D','Liposarcoma is the most common malignant soft tissue tumor in adults and favors the retroperitoneum and thigh. The diagnostic histological feature is the lipoblast — a cell with one or more lipid vacuoles indenting a scalloped hyperchromatic nucleus. Liposarcomas range from well-differentiated (low grade, resembling lipoma with scattered lipoblasts) to dedifferentiated, myxoid, and pleomorphic types. Lipoma contains only mature adipocytes without lipoblasts.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old presents with GI bleeding and a submucosal gastric mass. CT shows a 7 cm hypervascular mass. Biopsy shows spindle cells positive for CD117 (KIT) and DOG1. What is the diagnosis and the targeted therapy?','["A. Gastric adenocarcinoma; VEGF inhibitor (bevacizumab)","B. Gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST); imatinib (KIT/PDGFRA inhibitor)","C. Leiomyoma; no targeted therapy needed","D. Carcinoid tumor (NET); somatostatin analogue (octreotide)"]','A','GIST (gastrointestinal stromal tumor) is the most common mesenchymal tumor of the GI tract, arising from Cajal cells (intestinal pacemaker cells). The vast majority harbor activating KIT (CD117) mutations (80-85%) or PDGFRA mutations. IHC: CD117+, DOG1+, CD34+. Imatinib (a tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting KIT and PDGFRA) has revolutionized treatment of unresectable/metastatic GIST. Most GISTs occur in the stomach. Leiomyomas are smooth muscle tumors that lack CD117.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old presents with a painful deep soft tissue mass near the knee. MRI shows a well-defined mass with cystic areas. Histology shows a biphasic pattern of epithelial cells forming glands and spindle cells in fibrous stroma. Cytogenetics reveals t(X;18). What is the diagnosis?','["A. Synovial sarcoma","B. Fibrosarcoma","C. Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor","D. Epithelioid sarcoma"]','A','Synovial sarcoma (despite the name) rarely arises from synovial tissue. It typically occurs in young adults (15-40 years) near (but not always within) joints, especially the knee. The pathognomonic finding is t(X;18)(p11;q11) creating SS18-SSX1/SSX2 fusion. Histologically, the biphasic type shows epithelial cells (forming glands, positive for EMA/keratin) and spindle cells. The monophasic type consists of spindle cells only. It is locally aggressive with significant metastatic potential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','easy','mcq','Which immunohistochemical marker is most useful in confirming skeletal muscle differentiation in rhabdomyosarcoma?','["A. S100 protein","B. CD117 (KIT)","C. Desmin and myogenin","D. SMA (smooth muscle actin)"]','B','Desmin (intermediate filament of muscle cells) and myogenin (a myogenic transcription factor) are the most specific markers for skeletal muscle differentiation in rhabdomyosarcoma. MyoD1 is another useful marker. S100 marks neural/Schwann cells and melanocytes. CD117 (KIT) marks GISTs, mast cells, and stem cells. SMA marks smooth muscle (leiomyosarcoma) and myofibroblasts but not skeletal muscle specifically.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old woman with a large uterine mass refractory to GnRH therapy is found to have a high-grade sarcoma on pathology after hysterectomy. IHC shows SMA+, desmin+, but myogenin-negative. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Uterine leiomyosarcoma","B. Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma","C. Endometrial stromal sarcoma","D. Uterine carcinosarcoma"]','C','Leiomyosarcoma (LMS) arises from smooth muscle and most commonly occurs in the uterus and retroperitoneum. IHC shows positivity for smooth muscle markers (SMA, desmin, h-caldesmon) but is negative for skeletal muscle markers (myogenin, MyoD1). Uterine LMS is distinguished from benign leiomyoma by high mitotic rate (>10 mitoses/10 HPF), coagulative tumor cell necrosis, and significant cytological atypia. It carries poor prognosis. Rhabdomyosarcoma is myogenin-positive.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','medium','mcq','A 4-year-old girl presents with a pelvic mass and botryoid grape-like tissue protruding through the vagina. Biopsy shows a subepithelial layer of condensed rhabdomyoblasts (cambium layer). What is this specific subtype of rhabdomyosarcoma?','["A. Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma","B. Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma","C. Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, botryoid subtype","D. Spindle cell rhabdomyosarcoma"]','A','Botryoid rhabdomyosarcoma is a variant of embryonal RMS that grows into hollow organs (vagina, bladder, bile ducts), producing grape-like polypoid masses. The characteristic histological finding is the ''cambium layer'' — a condensed zone of rhabdomyoblasts directly beneath the mucosal epithelium. It carries the best prognosis among RMS variants. Alveolar RMS (PAX3/7-FOXO1 fusion) has the worst prognosis. Pleomorphic RMS occurs in adults.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','hard','mcq','Which genetic alteration distinguishes alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma from embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma and confers worse prognosis?','["A. t(X;18) creating SS18-SSX fusion","B. t(11;22) creating EWSR1-FLI1 fusion","C. PAX3-FOXO1 or PAX7-FOXO1 fusion from t(2;13) or t(1;13)","D. EWSR1-ATF1 fusion from t(12;22)"]','D','Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma (ARMS) is characterized by PAX3-FOXO1 fusion from t(2;13)(q35;q14) or PAX7-FOXO1 from t(1;13)(p36;q14). These fusion oncoproteins act as aberrant transcription factors driving tumor proliferation. ARMS has a worse prognosis than embryonal RMS (ERMS), which shows loss of heterozygosity at 11p15.5. t(11;22) is Ewing sarcoma; t(X;18) is synovial sarcoma; EWSR1-ATF1 is clear cell sarcoma.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Soft Tissue Tumours','medium','mcq','What is the most common malignant soft tissue tumor in adults?','["A. Osteosarcoma","B. Liposarcoma","C. Rhabdomyosarcoma","D. Synovial sarcoma"]','B','Liposarcoma is the most common malignant soft tissue tumor in adults, accounting for approximately 20% of all sarcomas. It most commonly arises in the retroperitoneum and thigh. Subtypes include well-differentiated (most common, lowest grade), myxoid (intermediate grade, t(12;16) FUS-DDIT3), dedifferentiated, and pleomorphic (highest grade). Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma in children. Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old sexually active woman presents with migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, and a pustular skin rash. Joint aspirate from the left knee grows gram-negative diplococci. What is the most likely causative organism?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Streptococcus pyogenes","C. Neisseria gonorrhoeae","D. Haemophilus influenzae"]','C','Neisseria gonorrhoeae is the most common cause of septic arthritis in sexually active young adults (age 15-40). It presents with the classic triad of migratory arthritis/arthralgia, tenosynovitis, and dermatitis (pustular or vesicular skin lesions). Blood cultures are often negative; the diagnosis is confirmed by cultures from genital, throat, or rectal sites. S. aureus is the most common cause in all other age groups. H. influenzae was common in children <2 years before vaccination.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','hard','mcq','A 10-year-old child develops acute hematogenous osteomyelitis of the distal femur metaphysis. Why does hematogenous osteomyelitis preferentially affect the metaphysis in children?','["A. The metaphysis has the thickest cortex, trapping bacteria after seeding","B. The metaphysis has sluggish capillary blood flow in sinusoidal vessels with few phagocytic cells, facilitating bacterial colonization","C. Metaphyseal bone has lower oxygen tension due to cartilaginous growth plate blocking diffusion","D. The metaphysis receives direct arterial blood supply from periosteal vessels making it vulnerable"]','B','The metaphyseal predilection of hematogenous osteomyelitis in children is explained by the unique vascular anatomy: terminal metaphyseal capillaries make sharp hairpin turns and drain into large sinusoidal veins. This creates sluggish, turbulent blood flow and a relative lack of phagocytic cells, allowing bacteria seeded from bacteremia to colonize and proliferate. After skeletal maturity when the growth plate closes, the vascular anatomy changes and subarticular/epiphyseal seeding becomes more common.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','easy','mcq','A patient with sickle cell disease develops osteomyelitis. Which organism is the most common cause of osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease (in addition to S. aureus)?','["A. Streptococcus pneumoniae","B. Salmonella species","C. Pseudomonas aeruginosa","D. Klebsiella pneumoniae"]','A','Salmonella species are characteristically associated with osteomyelitis in patients with sickle cell disease. Functional asplenia from repeated splenic infarctions impairs clearance of encapsulated organisms and Salmonella. While S. aureus remains the overall most common cause of osteomyelitis even in sickle cell patients, Salmonella is the classic and high-yield organism specifically associated with this condition. Pseudomonas is associated with osteomyelitis in IV drug users and puncture wounds through shoe soles.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','medium','mcq','X-ray of a patient with chronic osteomyelitis shows a dense fragment of devitalized bone surrounded by a granulation tissue cavity, with a shell of new periosteal bone around it. What are these two structures called?','["A. Tophi and involucrum","B. Brodie abscess and sequestrum","C. Sequestrum (dead bone) and involucrum (new bone shell)","D. Enchondroma and osteochondroma"]','C','In chronic osteomyelitis: (1) Sequestrum is a fragment of necrotic (dead) cortical bone that has been separated from viable bone by pus — it appears radiodense because it lacks remodeling. (2) Involucrum is the shell of new reactive periosteal bone formed around the infected area. The sequestrum acts as a nidus for ongoing infection and must be surgically removed (sequestrectomy) for cure. Brodie abscess is a localized chronic bone abscess without sequestrum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old diabetic patient presents with a non-healing foot ulcer and osteomyelitis on MRI. Which organism is the most common pathogen in diabetic foot osteomyelitis?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA)","B. Salmonella typhi","C. Pseudomonas aeruginosa","D. Streptococcus agalactiae"]','C','S. aureus (including MRSA) is the most common cause of diabetic foot osteomyelitis because the infection typically arises from contiguous spread from overlying skin ulcers where S. aureus colonizes. Diabetic foot infections are often polymicrobial (including streptococci, gram-negative rods, and anaerobes), but S. aureus is the most common single pathogen. Pseudomonas is classically associated with puncture wound osteomyelitis. Salmonella is associated with sickle cell disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','hard','mcq','A 40-year-old immigrant from Southeast Asia presents with chronic back pain. MRI spine shows destruction of two adjacent thoracic vertebral bodies with preservation of the intervertebral disc and a large paravertebral "cold abscess." What is the diagnosis?','["A. Pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis (discitis)","B. Spinal metastases from lung cancer","C. Pott''s disease (tuberculous spondylitis)","D. Ankylosing spondylitis with vertebral fracture"]','A','Pott''s disease is tuberculous osteomyelitis of the spine. TB characteristically spreads via the anterior longitudinal ligament to involve two adjacent vertebral bodies while initially sparing the disc (unlike pyogenic osteomyelitis which rapidly destroys the disc/end-plates). Paravertebral cold abscess (without redness/heat from indolent inflammation) and gibbus deformity (angular kyphosis) are characteristic. Lower thoracic/upper lumbar spine is most commonly affected. MRI is the investigation of choice.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','medium','mcq','A Brodie abscess is a form of chronic localized osteomyelitis. Which organism most commonly causes this condition?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Streptococcus pyogenes","C. Salmonella typhimurium","D. Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]','C','A Brodie abscess is a subacute/chronic form of osteomyelitis characterized by a well-defined lucent cavity (abscess) in the metaphysis surrounded by reactive sclerotic bone on X-ray. It most commonly results from S. aureus infection in which the organism is not completely eliminated and the host immune response walls off the infection. It typically occurs in the proximal or distal tibia metaphysis of children and adolescents. Treatment requires surgical curettage and antibiotics.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','easy','mcq','Which joint fluid finding is diagnostic of septic arthritis rather than other causes of acute joint inflammation?','["A. WBC >2000/mm3 with >75% neutrophils","B. Presence of crystals under polarized light microscopy","C. Positive Gram stain or culture of synovial fluid for bacteria","D. Turbid synovial fluid with reduced glucose"]','C','Positive synovial fluid culture (or Gram stain) is the definitive diagnosis of septic arthritis. While WBC >50,000/mm3 with >75% neutrophils and low glucose strongly suggest infection, none of these is diagnostic alone (crystal arthropathy and severe RA can have high WBCs). Only positive culture/Gram stain establishes the diagnosis. Crystal identification distinguishes gout/pseudogout. All suspected septic joints require aspiration and culture before antibiotics.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','medium','mcq','A neonate develops septic arthritis of the hip. Which organisms are most commonly responsible for septic arthritis in neonates?','["A. Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Staphylococcus aureus","B. Group B Streptococcus (S. agalactiae) and Staphylococcus aureus","C. Haemophilus influenzae and Streptococcus pneumoniae","D. Escherichia coli and Listeria monocytogenes"]','B','In neonates (first 30 days), septic arthritis and osteomyelitis are caused by Group B Streptococcus (GBS/S. agalactiae) and Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), reflecting perinatal transmission and skin/healthcare exposure. N. gonorrhoeae can also infect neonates (ophthalmia neonatorum and septic arthritis from birth canal passage). H. influenzae was previously common in unvaccinated children aged 2 months-2 years but is now rare due to Hib vaccination.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Septic Arthritis & Osteomyelitis','hard','mcq','What is the most common route of infection in hematogenous osteomyelitis in children, and which bone is most commonly affected?','["A. Direct inoculation from puncture wounds; calcaneus most common","B. Contiguous spread from adjacent soft tissue infection; skull most common","C. Hematogenous seeding of the metaphysis; distal femur and proximal tibia most common","D. Lymphatic spread from lymph node infection; vertebrae most common"]','D','In children, hematogenous osteomyelitis results from bacteremia seeding the metaphysis of long bones. The distal femur and proximal tibia are the most commonly affected sites, followed by the proximal femur and proximal humerus. The metaphyseal vascular anatomy (sluggish sinusoidal flow, hairpin capillary turns, few phagocytes) predisposes to bacterial colonization. Vertebral osteomyelitis from hematogenous spread is more common in adults. Contiguous and puncture-wound routes are distinct mechanisms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','easy','mcq','A 70-year-old man presents with bone pain and deformity. Serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is markedly elevated, but calcium and phosphate levels are normal. X-ray of the pelvis shows mixed lytic and sclerotic changes with cortical thickening. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Primary hyperparathyroidism","B. Osteomalacia","C. Paget''s disease of bone","D. Osteoblastic bone metastases from prostate cancer"]','C','Paget''s disease of bone is characterized by markedly elevated ALP (reflecting intense osteoblastic activity) with normal serum calcium and phosphate. This biochemical profile distinguishes it from hyperparathyroidism (elevated calcium) and osteomalacia (low calcium and phosphate, elevated PTH). Prostate metastases can be osteoblastic but are excluded by age context and the mixed lytic-sclerotic pattern on X-ray, which reflects the mixed phase of Paget''s.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','medium','mcq','A biopsy from pagetic bone shows irregular cement lines creating a "mosaic" or "jigsaw puzzle" pattern of lamellar bone. What is the pathological basis of this histological finding?','["A. Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals in bone matrix","B. Repeated cycles of osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic repair creating irregular cement lines between successive bone units","C. Periosteal ossification creating concentric rings of new bone","D. Woven bone replacing normal lamellar bone in a fibrous stroma"]','B','The mosaic pattern (jigsaw puzzle or cement-line pattern) is the histologic hallmark of Paget''s disease. It results from the chaotic and excessive remodeling — osteoclasts resorb irregular lacunae, which are then refilled by osteoblasts in a disorganized manner. Each cycle of resorption/repair leaves a cement line (reversal line), and the accumulated, haphazardly arranged bone units create the characteristic mosaic appearance. This distinguishes Paget''s from normal lamellar bone and woven bone (of other disorders).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','medium','mcq','A patient with Paget''s disease involving the skull develops progressive hearing loss and headaches. The skull X-ray shows marked thickening of the calvaria with a "cotton-wool" appearance. What causes the hearing loss in Paget''s disease?','["A. Sensorineural hearing loss from cochlear nerve compression by pagetic bone","B. Conductive hearing loss from ossicular chain fusion or cochlear involvement","C. Central hearing loss from brainstem compression","D. Both conductive and sensorineural components from bony encroachment on the cochlea and ossicles"]','D','Hearing loss in Paget''s disease of the skull results from bony encroachment on auditory structures. Conductive hearing loss occurs from deformity and thickening of the bones of the middle ear (ossicular chain), while sensorineural hearing loss occurs from compression of the cochlea or cochlear nerve by expanding pagetic bone. Both components are often present simultaneously. The thickened "hat-band" skull can also cause headaches, cranial nerve palsies, and basilar invagination.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','hard','mcq','A patient with extensive Paget''s disease of the pelvis and long bones develops high-output cardiac failure. What is the pathophysiological mechanism?','["A. Paget''s disease deposits calcium in coronary arteries causing ischemic heart disease","B. Extensive hypervascular pagetic bone creates arteriovenous shunts, increasing cardiac output to perfuse pagetic tissue","C. Anemia from marrow replacement requires increased cardiac output to compensate","D. Hypercalcemia in Paget''s disease causes cardiac dysfunction"]','B','High-output cardiac failure in Paget''s disease occurs because pagetic bone is highly vascular (it appears ''hot'' on bone scan). The intense neovascularization and increased blood flow through pagetic lesions — effectively acting as arteriovenous shunts — can dramatically increase total peripheral blood flow requirements. With extensive disease (>35% of skeleton), the increased cardiac output demand eventually exceeds cardiac reserve, leading to high-output cardiac failure. This is analogous to the mechanism in severe anemia or AV fistulas.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the most feared serious complication of Paget''s disease of bone?','["A. Pathological fracture through pagetic bone","B. Sarcomatous transformation (osteosarcoma)","C. Spinal cord compression from vertebral expansion","D. Hypercalcemia causing renal calculi"]','A','Sarcomatous transformation is the most feared complication of Paget''s disease, although it is rare (<1% of patients). Osteosarcoma is the most common sarcoma arising in pagetic bone, followed by fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma. It typically presents with sudden worsening of bone pain, a new soft tissue mass, and rapidly expanding lytic lesion on X-ray. The prognosis is very poor (5-year survival ~5%). While fractures and spinal cord compression are more common complications, sarcoma is the most dreaded.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','medium','mcq','Paget''s disease has three sequential histological phases. In which order do these phases occur?','["A. Sclerotic → mixed → lytic","B. Mixed → lytic → sclerotic","C. Lytic (osteoclastic) → mixed (osteoclastic-osteoblastic) → sclerotic (burned-out)","D. Lytic → sclerotic → mixed"]','D','Paget''s disease progresses through three phases: (1) Osteolytic phase — predominantly osteoclastic resorption, creating "blade of grass" lytic lesion in cortical bone or osteoporosis circumscripta in the skull; (2) Mixed phase — simultaneous osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic repair, creating the mixed lytic/sclerotic appearance with mosaic bone pattern; (3) Sclerotic (burned-out) phase — osteoclastic activity wanes, predominantly sclerotic bone with mosaic pattern remains. The natural history follows this lytic → mixed → sclerotic progression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','hard','mcq','A paramyxoviral etiology has been proposed for Paget''s disease. Which finding supports this hypothesis?','["A. Elevated serum IgG antibodies against paramyxovirus in all Paget''s patients","B. Paramyxovirus-like nuclear inclusions seen in osteoclast nuclei on electron microscopy","C. Resolution of Paget''s disease with antiviral therapy","D. Clustering of Paget''s cases in households suggesting human-to-human transmission"]','B','Electron microscopy of pagetic osteoclasts has revealed nuclear and cytoplasmic inclusions resembling paramyxovirus nucleocapsids (similar to measles, respiratory syncytial virus, or canine distemper virus). This morphological finding supported the viral hypothesis for Paget''s disease. Additionally, measles virus RNA has been detected in pagetic tissue by PCR. However, the viral etiology remains unproven, and no antiviral treatment has been shown to alter disease course. The disease responds to bisphosphonates.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','medium','mcq','A patient with Paget''s disease has bowing of the right tibia ("sabre tibia") causing abnormal gait. What is the pathological basis of bone deformity in Paget''s disease?','["A. Pagetic bone, though thickened, is structurally weaker due to its disorganized mosaic architecture and undergoes plastic deformation under normal weight-bearing","B. Inflammatory cytokines soften pagetic bone, allowing gravity-driven deformation","C. Osteoclastic resorption of cortical bone causes pathological fractures that heal in deformed position","D. Periosteal proliferation creates eccentric bone deposits that distort bone shape"]','C','Pagetic bone, despite being thicker and denser, is biomechanically inferior because its disorganized mosaic architecture lacks the structural alignment of normal lamellar bone. Under the normal stresses of weight-bearing, pagetic bone deforms by microscopic fractures ("chalk-stick" fractures on the convex cortex) and plastic deformation. The tibia, which bears significant compressive and bending forces, undergoes anterior bowing. The femur bows laterally. These deformities are caused by the bone''s mechanical weakness, not inflammation.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','easy','mcq','Which axial bones are most commonly affected by Paget''s disease?','["A. Cervical spine, clavicles, and sternum","B. Pelvis, spine (lumbar), skull, and femur","C. Ribs, scapulae, and thoracic spine","D. Radius, ulna, and small bones of the feet"]','A','Paget''s disease predominantly affects the axial skeleton: pelvis (most common single site — ~70%), lumbar vertebrae, skull, and proximal femur. Involvement can be monostotic (single bone) or polyostotic (multiple bones). The bones most commonly affected in descending order are: pelvis > spine > skull > femur > tibia. Small bones of the hands/feet and cervical spine are rarely affected. The distal appendicular skeleton (radius, ulna) is uncommonly involved.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Pathology','Paget''s Disease of Bone','medium','mcq','Which class of drugs is the treatment of choice for symptomatic Paget''s disease of bone, and what is their mechanism of action?','["A. Calcium and vitamin D supplements; promote normal bone mineralization","B. Bisphosphonates (zoledronate, risedronate); inhibit osteoclast function reducing abnormal bone turnover","C. Calcitonin injections; directly stimulate osteoblasts to form normal lamellar bone","D. Teriparatide (PTH analogue); intermittent PTH stimulates organized bone formation"]','B','Bisphosphonates are the treatment of choice for symptomatic Paget''s disease. They incorporate into bone matrix and inhibit osteoclast function (specifically inhibiting farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates), dramatically reducing the abnormal bone turnover. A single IV infusion of zoledronate can achieve remission for years. ALP levels normalize with treatment. Calcitonin is second-line; calcium/vitamin D are adjunctive; teriparatide is contraindicated in Paget''s (risk of sarcoma).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
